  "notes": {
   "series": "The shipped XGBoost regressor of this experiment's species and seed, on the notebook's 20 % validation split (train_test_split(test_size=0.2, random_state=42)): the rows it was not trained on. The selection applies to them; the training rows it selects are counted in split.n_training_excluded and not drawn.",
   "showers": "One shower per event, so one series. An event without a finite prediction or truth is counted per bin in n_missing and dropped, never zero-filled.",
   "own_bin": "Each event is placed in the producer's momentum bin (e1_bin) over the whole dataset, so the axis does not move with the selection.",
   "frames": "The model reads 52 event-level features of the absolute, per-shower translated and local frames at once, so the series has no input frame.",
   "calibration": "The regressor predicts the momentum scale directly; no sampling-fraction calibration is applied.",
   "whiskers": "Every whisker is the 95% interval of the aggregate it sits on (its interval is named in quantities), never the dispersion of the events in the bin. A bin of one event has a value and no whisker, and the median has none below N = 6.",
   "scale": "A point drives the y range from N = 15. Under-powered points above it are clipped, with their true values in y_max.overflow; with no driving point the range is provisional, set by point values only.",
   "angle": "θ is drawn as the networks' θ: residuals in mrad, a relative error |Δθ| / θ that grows like 1 / θ at small θ, the median |Δθ|, the smallest true θ and the count below 0.1 rad. φ is its own quantity group (phi_rmse, phi_mae, phi_median_abs): its residual is wrapped to (−π, π] and in mrad, with no relative error and no bias, because φ is an azimuth. The notebook's angle bars showed φ only; both are offered."
  },
